<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
    <!--
    This file contains suppressions for false positives identified by OWASP Dependency-Check.

    Each suppression should include:
    - A clear comment explaining why it's a false positive
    - The CVE or vulnerability ID
    - The affected dependency (GAV coordinates)
    - A notes field documenting the reason for suppression
    - An expiration date (until) to force periodic review

    Suppression format:
    <suppress>
       <notes><![CDATA[
       Explanation of why this is suppressed
       ]]></notes>
       <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/group\.id/artifact\-id@.*$</packageUrl>
       <cve>CVE-YYYY-NNNNN</cve>
       <until>YYYY-MM-DD</until>
    </suppress>

    For more information, see:
    https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/suppression.html
    -->

    <!-- Example suppression (remove this when adding real suppressions):
    <suppress>
       <notes><![CDATA[
       CVE-2023-12345 affects version 1.x but we use 2.x which has the fix.
       This is a false positive due to incorrect version detection.
       ]]></notes>
       <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/com\.example/example\-lib@.*$</packageUrl>
       <cve>CVE-2023-12345</cve>
       <until>2026-01-01</until>
    </suppress>
    -->

    <!--
    AWS CDK and Constructs Library Suppressions

    Note: The AWS CDK libraries are well-maintained by Amazon and receive regular security updates.
    Any CVEs found should be carefully evaluated before suppression. Only suppress if:
    1. The CVE affects a transitive dependency not used by CDK
    2. The CVE is fixed in the version we're using (version detection error)
    3. The CVE doesn't apply to our usage pattern
    -->

    <!--
    If you identify false positives during scanning, add them here following the format above.
    Always include:
    - Clear explanation in notes
    - Expiration date for periodic review
    - Specific CVE or vulnerability ID
    -->

    <!--
    AWS CDK Asset False Positives:

    The cdk-asset-awscli-v1 package bundles the AWS CLI as a Lambda layer which includes
    compressed JSON data files (endpoint-rule-set-1.json.gz, service-2.json.gz, etc.).
    The dependency scanner is incorrectly flagging these JSON data files as if they were
    vulnerable Ruby JSON gems or JavaScript packages.

    These are NOT actual code dependencies - they are data files used by the AWS CLI.
    The vulnerabilities CVE-2020-10663 (Ruby JSON gem) and CVE-2021-23497 (JavaScript
    @strikeentco/set package) do not apply to this project which is a Java/Maven project
    that uses AWS CDK.

    Verified: These are compressed JSON configuration files for AWS service endpoints,
    not executable code or library dependencies.
    -->

    <suppress until="2026-11-01Z">
       <notes><![CDATA[
       FALSE POSITIVE: CVE-2020-10663 affects the Ruby JSON gem, but this detection is
       from compressed JSON data files (endpoint-rule-set-1.json.gz, service-2.json.gz, etc.)
       inside the AWS CLI layer bundled with cdk-asset-awscli-v1.
       These are not Ruby gems or executable code - they are static configuration files.
       This project does not use Ruby or the Ruby JSON gem.
       ]]></notes>
       <filePath regex="true">.*cdk-asset-awscli-v1.*\.json\.gz$</filePath>
       <cve>CVE-2020-10663</cve>
    </suppress>

    <suppress until="2026-11-01Z">
       <notes><![CDATA[
       FALSE POSITIVE: CVE-2021-23497 affects the JavaScript package @strikeentco/set,
       but this detection is from compressed JSON data files (endpoint-rule-set-1.json.gz)
       inside the AWS CLI layer bundled with cdk-asset-awscli-v1.
       These are not JavaScript packages or executable code - they are static AWS service
       endpoint configuration files.
       This project does not use the @strikeentco/set npm package.
       ]]></notes>
       <filePath regex="true">.*cdk-asset-awscli-v1.*\.json\.gz$</filePath>
       <cve>CVE-2021-23497</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
    aws-cdk-lib bundled npm tooling (minimatch, yaml): real packages at real vulnerable
    versions, not a detection error, these ARE npm packages, not misidentified data files
    like the awscli suppressions above. Suppressed on criterion 3 (CVE doesn't apply to our
    usage pattern), based on tracing the actual bundled JS source in
    aws-cdk-lib-2.196.0.jsii.tgz, not assumption:

    - minimatch@3.1.2 has exactly two call sites in aws-cdk-lib core: GlobIgnoreStrategy
      (core/lib/fs/ignore.js), reached only via Code.fromAsset()/Source.asset()/
      BucketDeployment's `exclude` option, cloudforge-api never calls any of those APIs
      anywhere (every app here deploys from a pre-built Docker image, never local asset
      bundling), so this path is unreachable. And Stack's bundling-gate check
      (core/lib/stack.js), which matches CDK's own internal construct path against a
      context-supplied pattern list defaulting to ["**"], we never set BUNDLING_STACKS
      context, and the tested string is never attacker input.
    - yaml@1.10.2's real callers are core/lib/private/yaml-cfn.js and aws-eks/lib/cluster.js
      (Kubernetes manifest parsing). cloudforge-api uses neither EKS nor CfnInclude anywhere.

    Re-verified newer aws-cdk-lib releases don't fix this by version bump alone: checked
    2.196.0 (pinned), 2.196.1 (same-line patch), and 2.199.0 (latest available at suppression
    time), all three bundle the identical minimatch@3.1.2/yaml@1.10.2. Not a "we're behind"
    situation; AWS hasn't updated these internal build-tooling deps in any recent release.
    Bumping past 2.196.0 also isn't free regardless, see cdk.version's own pom.xml comment
    on why 2.197.0+ was deliberately avoided (nodejs22.x AwsCustomResource default,
    unavailable in some regions).
    -->

    <suppress until="2026-11-22Z">
       <notes><![CDATA[
       CVE doesn't apply to our usage: ReDoS requires attacker control over the glob
       *pattern* passed to minimatch, not just the tested string. Traced both real call
       sites in aws-cdk-lib core -- GlobIgnoreStrategy is only reached via CDK asset-bundling
       APIs (Code.fromAsset/Source.asset/BucketDeployment's exclude option) that
       cloudforge-api never calls; every application here deploys from a pre-built Docker
       image, never local file-asset bundling. See dependency-check-suppressions.xml's own
       header comment above this block for the full trace, including confirmation that newer
       aws-cdk-lib releases (checked through 2.199.0) still bundle this exact version.
       ]]></notes>
       <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:npm/minimatch@.*$</packageUrl>
       <cve>CVE-2026-26996</cve>
       <cve>CVE-2026-27903</cve>
       <cve>CVE-2026-27904</cve>
    </suppress>

    <suppress until="2026-11-22Z">
       <notes><![CDATA[
       CVE doesn't apply to our usage: requires the yaml library to actually parse
       attacker-supplied YAML text. Its real callers inside aws-cdk-lib
       (core/lib/private/yaml-cfn.js, aws-eks/lib/cluster.js) are CFN-YAML-template and EKS
       Kubernetes-manifest parsing -- cloudforge-api uses neither EKS nor CfnInclude anywhere.
       See dependency-check-suppressions.xml's own header comment above this block for the
       full trace, including confirmation that newer aws-cdk-lib releases (checked through
       2.199.0) still bundle this exact version.
       ]]></notes>
       <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:npm/yaml@.*$</packageUrl>
       <cve>CVE-2026-33532</cve>
    </suppress>

</suppressions>
